WO-20: vehicle-model contract + validation script (tools/validate_vehicle.py).

Formalizes what a designer must expose to add a vehicle, and checks a profile +
its model SDF against that contract BEFORE boot. Profiles are now self-describing
(each declares "model_sdf").

Contract checks (per domain): profile loads + passes constants validation; domain
in {surface,underwater} matching vehicle type {Rover,Sub}; AIS present for surface
/ null for underwater; fdm ports correct (relay 9002, gazebo port distinct and ==
the model's ArduPilotPlugin <fdm_port_in>); model SDF exists with an
ArduPilotPlugin, an IMU sensor, >=1 control channel; surface exposes navsat/GPS;
underwater declares buoyancy+hydrodynamics (model or world); every listed attack
is implemented or a known domain-N/A entry.

Results:
  wamv      -> ALL PASS (17 checks)
  bluerov2  -> ALL PASS (17 checks)
  _broken_test (deliberately malformed) -> 4 FAILURES correctly caught:
     domain/vehicle-type mismatch, ais-set-while-underwater, missing model SDF,
     unknown attack name. exit code 1.

So the validator is a real gate, not a rubber stamp -- a designer runs
`python3 tools/validate_vehicle.py <profile>` and fixes every FAIL before wiring
the vehicle into run_sim.sh / run_attack_suite.py.

WO-21 (prove the contract with a fully-built second surface vehicle) and WO-22
(optional second AUV) remain: the contract + validator (WO-20) are the reusable
scaffolding they plug into.
